Thanks.

35
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
All right, now moving on to loss aversion and prospect theory.

36
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
All right.

37
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:28,000
All right, now standard story of expect-utility theory is that in the real world people are risk averse.

38
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
This is an idea that behavioral economists have objected to, and they say, no, that's wrong.

39
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
People are actually something else that they call loss averse.

40
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Loss averse.

41
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
All right.

42
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:42,000
And what does this mean?

43
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
It means people don't like losing what they have.

44
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
All right, what's the difference between not liking risk and not liking loss?

45
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Well, so you know the old saying, it's better to have loved and lost than never to have loved at all.

46
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:01,000
All right, that's actually a lot like risk aversion, where the fact that you had it once and then you lose it, it's like, well, easy come, easy go, no big deal.

47
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Right.

48
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
But with loss aversion, it's not easy come, easy go.

49
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
Then it's like, I had something really good and then they took it from me and I've never been the same since.

50
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
It's like, well, you're no worse off than you were before you had it.

51
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
You went and ripped my heart out and showed it to me.

52
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
All right, bad idea.

53
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Okay, so anyway, in regular spec utility theory, there's no sense of losing things because there isn't any,

54
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
because what you have does not actually change the way you feel about stuff.

55
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Okay, so because in regular utility theory, preferences and endowments are separate things.

56
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
They've got nothing to do with each other.

57
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Whereas to talk about loss, you have to actually put your endowment somehow into the utility function.

58
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Right, make sense?

59
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Good.

60
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Okay, so anyway, so losing something you currently possess is worse than never having had it at all.

61
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Right, when you think about it, you cannot express in a normal utility theory where utility would just be a function of your stuff,

62
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
not be a function of your past stuff that you don't have anymore.

63
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
This is how you have to modify utility theory to make this work.

64
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
All right, now one common critique of behavioral economics,

65
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
all they do is poke holes in other people's stories and they don't come up with their own.

66
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
So here is their big thing that is actually a positive contribution,

67
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
at least tries to be a positive contribution.

68
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,000
All right, it's called prospect theory.

69
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
This is actual homo theory.

70
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:33,000
And the idea here is they want to go and replace a usual spec utility function,

71
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
which has a shape like this, like u is equal to w, square root of w.

72
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
This is a normal risk-averse utility function.

73
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Right, you'll notice how we've got this nice convexity here.

74
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
All right, let's see.

75
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Hold on, I'm just teaching convexity to KB, my son, so now I'm pretty darn good at it.

