00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Yeah, so.

77
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
It's his next son, KB.

78
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Yeah, yeah, so when the line is below the function, then that is convex.

79
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Yes, convex, of course.

80
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
Yes, convexity is when you want to mix things.

81
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Okay, that's right.

82
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
All right, so in calculus they have like concave upwards and downwards,

83
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
which is really messing me up.

84
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,000
All right, anyway, so yeah, so we've got this nice function here where you risk-averse

85
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
and therefore you would like, prefer a sure thing to the gamble,

86
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
prefer a sure thing to the gamble, sure thing to the same expected value.

87
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Okay, now this is the usual thing.

88
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
All right, so this is just regular.

89
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
All right, so regular spec utility with risk-aversion.

90
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
And prospect theory does something else.

91
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
It says, now, here's what people are really like.

92
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
What people are really like is they've got an S-shaped curve that kinks at the current reference point.

93
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
All right, S-shaped curve that kinks at the current reference point.

94
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
So you are actually, so you see how that's kind of like an S?

95
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
It's not that S-like, but yes, and then it has this kink here, right?

96
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
And that's normally thought of as, you know, that's your reference point.

97
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
This is basically where your endowments start.

98
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
So notice, so above that, then you've got a normal risk-averse utility function.

99
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
So that means that when you're above this point, then you want,

100
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:02,000
you prefer to get the sure thing with the same expected value as the gamble.

101
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
But if you happen to be below the risk of point, then you become risk-seeking.

102
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Then you become risk-seeking, and then you're there to say,

103
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
oh my God, it's not acceptable to go and lose anything.

104
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:18,000
I'd rather take a risk of everything, but have a chance of getting back to where I originally started.

105
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,000
So I really don't want to go and fall below my special magic point.

106
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,000
So what exactly is determining the risk?

107
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Well, of course, in an experiment, it's just going to be a piece of paper says you start here, and that's it.

108
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Now in the real world, it's supposed to be some kind of psychological idea of where you're supposed to be.

109
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Now, is that like what you have today?

110
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Or is it like what you imagine your station in life entitles you to?

111
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
That's kind of it, actually.

112
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
So much of this stuff is clearly defined in experiments.

113
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:56,000
And then when you try to apply it to the real world, then it gets kind of hazy, unfortunately.

114
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Yes?

115
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
You said below the reference point, you're risk-seeking.

116
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Yes.

117
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Okay, so would this be like gamblers who go into the casino with $100, and they lose a little bit of that money,

118
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
and they're like, oh, I've got to get back to the money.

119
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Yeah, exactly. I've got to get back to the money.

120
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,000
I can't go home with losses.

121
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
That would be so terrible.

122
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
That would be so terrible.

123
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Okay.

124
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,000
So your risk averse in games, like a normal person, but you're risk-seeking in losses.

125
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
So you get a choice of an extra $1,000 or a gamble of the same value.

126
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:25,000
They want what?

127
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
So you can either have an extra $1,000 for sure, or you can have a gamble of the same expected value.

128
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
What do these prospect theory people want?

129
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Extra $1,000 for sure or a gamble.

130
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Which one do they want?

131
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,000
For sure.

132
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,000
For sure.

133
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:44,000
However, if it's a chance of losing $1,000 for sure or a gamble of the same expected value, which one do they want to do?

134
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,000
Gamble.

135
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,000
They want to gamble.

136
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:47,000
Yes, that is the idea.

137
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:55,000
All right. So this is the simple version of prospect theory, and this is a classic case of a new theory that is reverse engineered to solve some problems,

138
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,000
but then it has a bunch of new problems, and in the end, like, gee, what do we do with all this work for?

139
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
So here's one really bad thing about it.

140
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:05,000
All right.

141
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:10,000
So according to this model, when do people want to buy – what kind of insurance policies do people want?

142
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:15,000
Do people want to buy insurance policies for gains or losses, if this model is true?

143
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Gains.

144
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Gains. All right.

145
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:23,000
Now, what are all the insurance industries based upon insuring gains?

146
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Hmm.

147
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Hmm. I can't think of any.

148
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
That's bad.

149
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
All right.

150
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:36,000
What do all known insurance products, at least all the ones that normal people have heard of, insure against?

151
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Losses.

152
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Losses.

153
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:39,000
And prospect theory is right.

154
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:40,000
People – no one would buy those policies.

155
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
So no one would want fire insurance.

156
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
No one would want health insurance.

157
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
No one would want life insurance.

158
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
So what kind of a theory is that?

159
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:49,000
That's stupid.

160
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:50,000
All right.

161
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
So it predicts the existence of a bunch of insurance products that don't exist,

162
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and predicts the nonexistence of all the insurance products that do exist.

163
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,000
All right.

164
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
So it solves some experimental anomalies, but other than that, it doesn't seem too good.

165
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,000
All right.

166
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:11,000
And then it took a little while before behavioral economists realized, oh, wait, yeah, that devastating objection.

167
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
Based upon the entire risk industry as we know it.

168
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,000
All right.

169
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:16,000
And then they said, all right, fine, fine.

170
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
We're going to do the fourfold theory.

171
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:19,000
All right.

172
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:24,000
So if you ever heard of epicycles, right, where you keep adding extra parts of the theory to go

173
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
and desperately try to avoid it anyway.

174
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,000
So here is the advanced, the fourfold version of prospect theory,

175
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:36,000
and it says that people are all of the four things, all the following four things.

176
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Risk averse and gains with high probabilities.

177
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
All right, risk averse and gains with high probabilities.

178
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:46,000
So that means that if you have like a 90% chance of getting a gain,

179
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:52,000
then you would be interested in going insuring to make sure that it's not, to turn it into 100.

180
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:53,000
All right.

181
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
But then you're going to be risk seeking gains with low probabilities.

182
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:02,000
You only have like a 1% chance of getting a gain, or you could go and have, or get the expected value,

183
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
then you want the 1% chance.

184
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,000
So like item two is designed to explain why people buy lottery tickets.

185
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
Lottery tickets have what, high or low probability of paying off in the network?

186
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,000
Remember.

187
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:13,000
Yes, love.

188
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:14,000
All right.

189
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
So this is, that's why people buy lottery tickets.

190
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
And then we got risk seeking and losses, risk averse and loss with low probabilities.

191
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:25,000
And number three is the proviso that explains all known insurance products.

192
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
And then finally you got risk seeking and losses with high probabilities.

193
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:33,000
This is like, either it's like a 90% chance of a loss or 100%,

194
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:38,000
and then you'd rather go and do the 90% one because then there's a chance you don't lose anything.

195
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
All right.

196
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:45,000
So anyway, if you think, you know, so in a lab you can go and set it up to try to make this work.

197
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,000
But, you know, the problem is that this is a theory with so much wiggle room, meaning like,

198
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:55,000
so notice just in those words, let's see, so the high and the low probabilities,

199
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
so not only do we now have wiggle room for what counts as a gain or a loss,

200
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
which is itself pretty vague, right?

201
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:07,000
You know, like if you missed your lunch today, is that a loss or a failure of a gain?

202
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,000
You know, again, you can set up an experiment where you say your loss is this, your gain is that,

203
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and then you can say, see, the model perfectly describes these very little behaviors.

204
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,000
But you're trying to take it out into the world.

205
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
It's ambiguous what counts as a gain or a loss, right?

206
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Because as you may know from small kids, a lot of it is just how they perceive it.

207
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:31,000
So like I said, if you want to go and lend something to a child without having them explode at you when you take it back,

208
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
you tell them, this is mine.

209
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
I am lending it to you.

210
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:41,000
In an hour you will give it back to me because it was never, ever yours.

211
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:47,000
It was an act of kindness that I allowed you to use what is mine, right?

212
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
And that reduced the chance the child would get really mad, right?

213
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:57,000
But this means that whether or not the child saw it as his or not, all that depends upon his own perception, right?

214
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:01,000
As well as how society tries to define what that perception is.

215
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
So is losing a job, is that a loss or not?

216
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Well, in a society where people feel entitled to their jobs, you might say that's a loss.

217
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:12,000
On the other hand, if people feel like it's just like a contract and easy come, easy go,

218
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:19,000
then again, they totally vary from person to person as to how you feel about it, right?

219
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,000
And then similarly, it also doesn't explain how we decide what counts as a low probability or high probability.

220
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,000
Again, like an experiment you can like do 90 and 10 and then alright, fine, that's definitely what we do.

221
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
90 is definitely high, 10 is definitely low.

222
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
But again, the real world, what counts?

223
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:38,000
It's like a 1% chance of getting guillotined, is that high or low?

224
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:41,000
And which theory would apply?

225
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
Alright, so questions about prospect theory?

226
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Alright, much beloved.

227
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:52,000
And you'll see why because it is a time where the evil economist Dr. Exow tried to say how things really are

228
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:56,000
rather than just pocket holes in other people's theories.

229
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:01,000
Alright, then let's go and do intertemporal anomalies.

230
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
Okay, alright, now let's see.

231
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
So, alright, now we've got perfect capital markets,

232
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,000
then basic microeconomics says that everyone will discount future payments by the rate of interest.

233
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Why?

234
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
Well, suppose that you are more impatient than the rate of interest.

235
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:20,000
What should you have already done with perfect capital markets?

236
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
You've got perfect capital markets, right?

237
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:26,000
And you have a discount rate of 5% but the market interest rate is 4%.

238
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
What should have already happened?

239
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:33,000
You should have already borrowed until you have actually equated the marginal values.

240
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Or similarly, suppose the market interest rate is 10% and your discount rate is 4%.

241
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:45,000
You should have already lent out so that you have equated your marginal value with the market interest rate.

242
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:52,000
Alright, now even with imperfect capital markets there are some definite predictions of interpersonal choice.

243
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:56,000
So, and behavioral economists have uncovered a number of these.

244
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:03,000
Alright, so here's one of the funnest ones which is borne out in real life right here in George Mason,

245
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:04,000
as I'll shortly explain.

246
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
And this is that sometimes people seem to have negative discount rates

247
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:13,000
even though there are plenty of ways to lend money out at positive interest.

248
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Alright, and what would this be like?

249
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Well, this is like choosing to be paid over 12 months rather than 9.

250
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Alright, so someone says, would you rather be paid your whole annual salary in the next 12 months

251
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:29,000
or should we spread it out over 12 months to make sure you have enough money?

252
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Well, what is that like?

253
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
It's like turning down free interest.

254
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:40,000
So, what can you do to make sure you have money in months 10 through 12?

255
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Save it.

256
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:46,000
While you're saving it, what would happen in a normal capitalist economy?

257
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
It would earn interest.

258
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,000
So, the fact that someone says, oh no, no, no, no, don't give me that money yearly.

259
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
Take a mixture, I don't get it until I'm ready for it.

260
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
That seems to indicate a negative discount rate.

261
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:03,000
And actually, when I came here to George Mason, faculty used to have the choice of being paid over 9 months or 12.

262
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
And I chose 9, like all good economists.

263
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,000
And then, the university eventually decided that they were going to get rid of that

264
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and they had to grandfather in all of the old timers who wanted to be paid over 9 months.

265
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:20,000
So, I'm one of a dying breed of people being paid over 9 months.

266
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:24,000
And I think it was Pete Bedke who went to some of the administration and said, why are you doing this?

267
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:31,000
They said, well, because the only people on campus who are choosing to be paid over 9 months are you economists.

268
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
So, we didn't see the point of having this system.

269
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:40,000
And I was like, oh, well, geez, the other faculty here are dumber than we thought.

270
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:47,000
But, of course, we're not dumb, we're just doing something that's hard to explain.

271
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,000
Or, here's another one.

272
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
People who persistently receive tax refunds.

273
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
How many people are glad when they get a tax refund?

274
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:01,000
Not seeming to realize there's something they could have done that would have been much better than getting a tax refund.

275
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
What can you do instead of getting a tax refund?

276
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
Save your money.

277
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
Just not pay the taxes in the first place.

278
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:12,000
And, Remy, what interest rate does the IRS pay on your overpayments again?

279
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
Zero nominal.

280
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,000
So, really what you want to do is, in fact, to be underpaid.

281
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:25,000
Now, you don't want to underpay maximally because the IRS does have a method for crushing people to do that.

282
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Although, it is a pretty low, low borrow.

283
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:37,000
So, I think it's if you're, let's see, so if the amount you owe exceeds 10% of your total tax bill, two years in a row, then there's a fine.

284
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:48,000
So, basically, if you were high precision, you want to basically alternate between paying the bare minimum and then underpaying by 9%.

285
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:51,000
And then just toggle that back and forth forever.

286
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:58,000
So, that, and again, it's because otherwise you're giving the IRS interest-free loans with your money.

287
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
So, who wants to do that?

288
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
So, anyway, those are two cases that are rock solid.

289
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
There's no way that you can get around.

290
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:10,000
I can't explain those other than by other than people are just giving out free money.

291
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,000
Yes?

292
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:15,000
So, if you are aware of your self-compensatory problem.

293
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
Yeah, yeah, we're getting to that.

294
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
It could serve as a mean mandamus.

295
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:21,000
Yes, yes.

296
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:31,000
So, if somehow the money just spends itself, it's in your hand, you're like, no, don't buy it, no.

297
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:35,000
Then you might want to have it.

298
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Now, there's also some evidence which is not as rock solid saying that consumers sometimes have discount rates far in excess of the market interest rate.

299
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:49,000
So, at least there are some studies claiming that people buy appliances.

300
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
They greatly underrate the cost savings.

301
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:56,000
So, that's not as totally solid, but at least that is the claim.

302
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:01,000
Anytime someone looks and says, oh, well, it saves a lot of energy, but I can't afford to buy this thing.

303
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:05,000
It's like, do you not have a credit card or something?

304
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:16,000
And if you've got a credit card and the interest rate is 10% on that, then if the energy savings would give you a better than 10% return, it would make sense to use the credit card.

305
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:21,000
If you've got a home equity line of credit, then you should be willing to borrow against that.

306
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:29,000
Or if you've got any kind of home equity, then normally you can borrow against that at exceedingly low rates.

307
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:33,000
And then also there's some evidence that people have different discount rates for gains versus losses.

308
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:39,000
So, in particular, there are some experiments that involve two hypotheticals.

309
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:44,000
One is if you could have a kiss from your favorite movie star this week or next week, which one would you want it?

310
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:51,000
And a lot of people say they want it next week because they want to spend the week going, oh, I'm going to get to kiss my favorite movie star.

311
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
Oh, it's going to be so great.

312
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:57,000
Whereas if it happens right now, then it's like, oh, well, then he'll look forward to it.

313
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:15,000
And on the other hand, there's also...


1
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:04,800
I think it's the same experiment like if you get a shot right now or in a week.

2
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:08,280
A lot of people say give it to me in a week so I'm not sweating over this.

3
00:20:08,280 --> 00:20:13,920
This is the one that I relate to best because whenever I test scores I would be waving at

4
00:20:13,920 --> 00:20:17,120
the minute that they're available or now when my kids test scores are ready.

5
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:19,720
Oh God, oh God, come on.

6
00:20:19,720 --> 00:20:22,160
All right, so that's that.

7
00:20:22,160 --> 00:20:27,280
All right, and then now we start getting into the really weird stuff.

8
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:32,360
So there's also evidence that discount rates vary with the total amount of waiting time.

9
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:38,080
All right, so people discount a year and a day versus a day or versus a year as virtually

10
00:20:38,080 --> 00:20:40,800
the same which is what normal theory predicts, right?

11
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:45,520
366 days worth of interest, 365 days of interest, barely a difference.

12
00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:49,960
But the people discount tomorrow way more than today.

13
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:55,880
All right, now notice what will happen if someone says hey do you want it in a year

14
00:20:55,880 --> 00:20:58,440
or a year and a day and he says I don't really care.

15
00:20:58,440 --> 00:21:01,080
All right, now 365 days pass.

16
00:21:01,080 --> 00:21:05,240
All right, now is the choice between today and tomorrow.

17
00:21:05,240 --> 00:21:09,900
And now a lot of people no longer say I don't care.

18
00:21:09,900 --> 00:21:15,600
But what is one thing we know about what will occur in 365 days?

19
00:21:15,600 --> 00:21:19,120
Something 366 days off will turn into something one day away, right?

20
00:21:19,120 --> 00:21:22,200
That's basic math of time.

21
00:21:22,200 --> 00:21:26,680
Okay, so if people have these preferences it means that there's actually not just an

22
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:29,960
intransitivity, there's an irreflexivity.

23
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:33,080
There's a predictable flipping of what you'll want.

24
00:21:33,080 --> 00:21:37,160
So you can imagine that someone say, today someone says hey do you want to have fish

25
00:21:37,160 --> 00:21:40,040
or steak for lunch?

26
00:21:40,040 --> 00:21:41,040
And you say fish.

27
00:21:41,040 --> 00:21:44,040
And the day happens and they say hey do you want to change your mind?

28
00:21:44,040 --> 00:21:45,040
Well of course.

29
00:21:45,040 --> 00:21:46,040
All right, steak.

30
00:21:46,040 --> 00:21:49,040
It's like well then why didn't you just anticipate what you were going to say?

31
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:54,520
It's like well I didn't feel that way yesterday but, but of course it's just a random thing,

32
00:21:54,520 --> 00:21:55,520
no big deal.

33
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:58,600
But if it happens reliably then there's something strange, right?

34
00:21:58,600 --> 00:22:02,760
It's like every time I ask what he's going to want tomorrow he says A. And whenever tomorrow

35
00:22:02,760 --> 00:22:05,480
arrives he says he doesn't want A anymore.

36
00:22:05,480 --> 00:22:07,480
This is typical of many small children.

37
00:22:07,480 --> 00:22:14,240
It's one of their more annoying features is changing their mind in a predictable way.

38
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:15,240
Okay.

39
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:26,560
All right and this implies what is called a dynamic inconsistency.

40
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:32,080
So as time goes on people predictably regret whatever they chose initially.

41
00:22:32,080 --> 00:22:38,200
And people often see this as some sign of some kind of a self-control problem.

42
00:22:38,200 --> 00:22:41,880
A self-control problem.

43
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:42,880
Okay.

44
00:22:42,880 --> 00:22:44,140
All right.

45
00:22:44,140 --> 00:22:45,720
So you know like class example.

46
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:51,400
So you want to save for retirement in the abstract but every day you want to buy stuff,

47
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:52,400
right?

48
00:22:52,400 --> 00:22:56,400
Or you want to be thin in the abstract but every day you want to eat tasty food.

49
00:22:56,400 --> 00:22:57,400
Baconators.

50
00:22:57,400 --> 00:22:58,400
Yes.

51
00:22:58,400 --> 00:22:59,400
Right?

52
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:00,400
And so on.

53
00:23:00,400 --> 00:23:01,400
Right?

54
00:23:01,400 --> 00:23:04,280
So or like you want to be in the abstract you want to be physically fit but each day

55
00:23:04,280 --> 00:23:07,120
you want to watch TV and not go to the gym.

56
00:23:07,120 --> 00:23:08,120
Okay.

57
00:23:08,120 --> 00:23:09,120
All right.

58
00:23:09,120 --> 00:23:13,280
And then the idea is that a person might want to have their money taken from them, right?

59
00:23:13,280 --> 00:23:14,480
In order to overcome temptation.

60
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:18,080
Or you might want to not have any tasty food in your house so you just have to stock with

61
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:19,080
salad.

62
00:23:19,080 --> 00:23:20,080
And then great.

63
00:23:20,080 --> 00:23:21,080
Okay.

64
00:23:21,080 --> 00:23:25,560
So now like you don't feel like you don't feel a temptation because all I have in front

65
00:23:25,560 --> 00:23:27,560
of me is salad.

66
00:23:27,560 --> 00:23:28,560
Okay.

67
00:23:28,560 --> 00:23:30,000
All right.

68
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:33,560
So this is one of the most popular products of behavioral economics and it resonates with

69
00:23:33,560 --> 00:23:34,560
a lot of people.

70
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:41,760
But at the same time let me just suggest it's much more confusing and less clear than people

71
00:23:41,760 --> 00:23:42,760
would have you believe.

72
00:23:42,760 --> 00:23:43,760
All right.

73
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:47,400
Like one thing to consider is why assume that the long run plan is the real plan and the

74
00:23:47,400 --> 00:23:49,200
short run plan is the fake plan.

75
00:23:49,200 --> 00:23:50,200
Right?

76
00:23:50,200 --> 00:23:52,000
Maybe it's the other way around.

77
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:56,400
So maybe the real you is the person that doesn't like exercising and enjoys eating.

78
00:23:56,400 --> 00:23:59,120
And the fake you is the person writing New Year's resolutions.

79
00:23:59,120 --> 00:24:00,120
All right.

80
00:24:00,120 --> 00:24:05,200
Because like you know how many days of the year are you the short run person?

81
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:08,280
364 days of the year.

82
00:24:08,280 --> 00:24:10,600
How many days of the year are you this long run person?

