01:10:39,160 --> 01:10:41,360
So now we will continue our journey

247
01:10:41,360 --> 01:10:44,160
through people's cognitive failings

248
01:10:44,160 --> 01:10:46,760
when we are ourselves somewhat tired.

249
01:10:46,760 --> 01:10:50,560
But does an economist care about being tired?

250
01:10:50,560 --> 01:10:53,160
No!

251
01:10:53,160 --> 01:10:54,560
We are not daunted by this.

252
01:10:54,560 --> 01:10:56,360
We are not subject to these normal constraints.

253
01:10:56,360 --> 01:10:59,360
And so we will proceed at the full height of our abilities

254
01:10:59,360 --> 01:11:01,560
as we look down upon the failures of others.

255
01:11:01,560 --> 01:11:02,960
All right.

256
01:11:02,960 --> 01:11:04,360
All right.

257
01:11:04,360 --> 01:11:07,560
So, availability and representative-ist biases,

258
01:11:07,560 --> 01:11:10,760
two of the ones that economists have talked about the most.

259
01:11:10,760 --> 01:11:11,960
All right.

260
01:11:11,960 --> 01:11:14,760
So, people often estimate probabilities

261
01:11:14,760 --> 01:11:16,960
based upon the ease of thinking of examples.

262
01:11:16,960 --> 01:11:19,960
And this is known as the availability heuristic.

263
01:11:19,960 --> 01:11:20,960
The availability heuristic.

264
01:11:20,960 --> 01:11:23,560
So, can I think of examples easily?

265
01:11:23,560 --> 01:11:25,360
How many examples can I think of?

266
01:11:25,360 --> 01:11:26,760
If you can't think of many, then you say,

267
01:11:26,760 --> 01:11:28,760
well, then I guess it must be unlikely.

268
01:11:28,760 --> 01:11:29,760
Right, on the other hand, you know,

269
01:11:29,760 --> 01:11:31,560
if you can think of a lot of examples really easily,

270
01:11:31,560 --> 01:11:33,760
then it must be likely.

271
01:11:33,760 --> 01:11:34,760
Okay.

272
01:11:34,760 --> 01:11:36,760
So, you know, like people with dogs.

273
01:11:36,760 --> 01:11:37,760
All right.

274
01:11:37,760 --> 01:11:38,760
Well, you can think of a lot of people with dogs,

275
01:11:38,760 --> 01:11:41,760
so probably high probability of people with dogs.

276
01:11:41,760 --> 01:11:44,760
People with chinchillas.

277
01:11:44,760 --> 01:11:45,760
Hmm.

278
01:11:45,760 --> 01:11:46,760
Can't think of anybody like that.

279
01:11:46,760 --> 01:11:50,360
So, probably the odds that the person has one is low.

280
01:11:50,360 --> 01:11:51,360
All right.

281
01:11:51,360 --> 01:11:53,760
Now, sometimes this is a useful heuristic.

282
01:11:53,760 --> 01:11:55,760
In fact, probably a lot of times it's a useful heuristic.

283
01:11:55,760 --> 01:11:58,760
Can I think of any time that it's ever occurred?

284
01:11:58,760 --> 01:12:00,760
No, well, then it probably hardly ever occurs.

285
01:12:00,760 --> 01:12:02,760
All right.

286
01:12:02,760 --> 01:12:05,760
So, but it does sometimes generate

287
01:12:05,760 --> 01:12:07,760
reliably biased estimates.

288
01:12:07,760 --> 01:12:10,760
In particular, if things are vivid or memorable,

289
01:12:10,760 --> 01:12:12,760
things are vivid or memorable,

290
01:12:12,760 --> 01:12:14,760
then we tend to overestimate probabilities.

291
01:12:14,760 --> 01:12:17,760
So, the main one that's been done experimentally

292
01:12:17,760 --> 01:12:19,760
is pretty dry, and it says, well,

293
01:12:19,760 --> 01:12:21,760
are there more words in the dictionary

294
01:12:21,760 --> 01:12:22,760
that start with the letters A

295
01:12:22,760 --> 01:12:24,760
or have the word, where the letter I

296
01:12:24,760 --> 01:12:26,760
is the third to last letter in the word?

297
01:12:26,760 --> 01:12:28,760
All right, so they still, so,

298
01:12:28,760 --> 01:12:30,760
probability that a randomly selected word in the dictionary

299
01:12:30,760 --> 01:12:32,760
starts with A, versus probability

300
01:12:32,760 --> 01:12:34,760
that a randomly selected word in the dictionary

301
01:12:34,760 --> 01:12:36,760
has I as the third to last letter.

302
01:12:36,760 --> 01:12:39,760
Can you think of a lot of words that start with A?

303
01:12:39,760 --> 01:12:40,760
Yes.

304
01:12:40,760 --> 01:12:42,760
Can you think of a bunch of words

305
01:12:42,760 --> 01:12:44,760
that have I as the third to last letter?

306
01:12:44,760 --> 01:12:45,760
Yes.

307
01:12:45,760 --> 01:12:47,760
Well, if you've got the trick, then it's easy.

308
01:12:47,760 --> 01:12:49,760
If you're like singing, dancing, running,

309
01:12:49,760 --> 01:12:51,760
if you remember that the ing suffix

310
01:12:51,760 --> 01:12:54,760
will give you an infinite number of words like that,

311
01:12:54,760 --> 01:12:56,760
at least a ton of words like that, you're fine,

312
01:12:56,760 --> 01:12:58,760
but if you don't notice that,

313
01:12:58,760 --> 01:12:59,760
if that doesn't dawn on you,

314
01:12:59,760 --> 01:13:01,760
then you might have trouble thinking of any words

315
01:13:01,760 --> 01:13:03,760
with I as the third to last letter.

316
01:13:03,760 --> 01:13:05,760
All right?

317
01:13:05,760 --> 01:13:07,760
Okay, so anyway, so,

318
01:13:07,760 --> 01:13:08,760
what you would do in an experiment

319
01:13:08,760 --> 01:13:09,760
would be something like,

320
01:13:09,760 --> 01:13:11,760
you take half the people in the room,

321
01:13:11,760 --> 01:13:12,760
and half the people just say,

322
01:13:12,760 --> 01:13:14,760
you say, all right, go and make a list

323
01:13:14,760 --> 01:13:15,760
as many words that you can think of

324
01:13:15,760 --> 01:13:17,760
that start with the letter A,

325
01:13:17,760 --> 01:13:18,760
as many words you can think of as words

326
01:13:18,760 --> 01:13:20,760
with the letter I in the third to last position,

327
01:13:20,760 --> 01:13:22,760
and then over in the second room,

328
01:13:22,760 --> 01:13:24,760
