01:23:35,260 --> 01:23:40,460
All right. So, there's a classic experiment along these lines,

95
01:23:40,460 --> 01:23:43,980
which asks you to begin with imagining something very odd.

96
01:23:43,980 --> 01:23:47,260
This is an engineering and psychology conference.

97
01:23:47,260 --> 01:23:50,220
So, it's a conference where the people there are,

98
01:23:50,220 --> 01:23:52,860
engineers and psychologists getting together to talk

99
01:23:52,860 --> 01:23:54,980
about whatever they would like to talk about.

100
01:23:54,980 --> 01:23:57,140
Okay. All right.

101
01:23:57,140 --> 01:23:59,940
And then here is the key part of the experiment.

102
01:23:59,940 --> 01:24:03,340
Some people are told that 70 percent of the attendees are engineers,

103
01:24:03,340 --> 01:24:06,260
others are told 30 percent of the attendees are engineers.

104
01:24:06,260 --> 01:24:09,340
All right. And then, you go and say,

105
01:24:09,340 --> 01:24:11,180
all right, so suppose you meet a person who has

106
01:24:11,180 --> 01:24:13,420
warm room glasses and a pocket protector,

107
01:24:13,420 --> 01:24:18,580
which is stereotypical wardrobe for what,

108
01:24:18,580 --> 01:24:20,140
an engineer or psychologist?

109
01:24:20,140 --> 01:24:20,700
Engineer.

110
01:24:20,700 --> 01:24:22,740
Yes. So, my dad was an engineer in the 80s,

111
01:24:22,740 --> 01:24:24,020
and he did have warm room glasses,

112
01:24:24,020 --> 01:24:25,540
and he did wear a pocket protector.

113
01:24:25,540 --> 01:24:27,500
All right. Now, the other like stereotypical clothes for

114
01:24:27,500 --> 01:24:30,180
a psychology professor would be like a tweed jacket and a pipe.

115
01:24:30,180 --> 01:24:31,300
Okay.

116
01:24:31,300 --> 01:24:32,460
Frazier Crane.

117
01:24:32,460 --> 01:24:33,780
Yes. And?

118
01:24:33,780 --> 01:24:34,820
Frazier Crane.

119
01:24:34,820 --> 01:24:35,500
Yeah. Frazier Crane.

120
01:24:35,500 --> 01:24:36,020
Yeah.

121
01:24:36,020 --> 01:24:37,420
Did he have a pipe?

122
01:24:37,420 --> 01:24:38,900
In some episodes, I think.

123
01:24:38,900 --> 01:24:40,740
Oh, he did. All right.

124
01:24:40,740 --> 01:24:43,060
Now, the striking thing is that in an experiment,

125
01:24:43,060 --> 01:24:44,580
you seem to get very similar answers

126
01:24:44,580 --> 01:24:47,620
regardless of whether you tell people the base rate is 70 and 30.

127
01:24:47,620 --> 01:24:49,740
Now, this would be okay if people were just

128
01:24:49,740 --> 01:24:51,620
saying 100 percent probability of an engineer,

