01:27:40,860 --> 01:27:42,260
All right.

182
01:27:42,260 --> 01:27:45,060
So, yes, so these are other cases of this problem.

183
01:27:45,060 --> 01:27:46,860
And I was mentioning gaydar.

184
01:27:46,860 --> 01:27:52,460
So this is another great example of the representative misbias where,

185
01:27:52,460 --> 01:27:56,860
so, like, you may think of pink as a gay color.

186
01:27:56,860 --> 01:28:00,260
And yet how many, but you know, like, but you start with the base rate.

187
01:28:00,260 --> 01:28:06,060
So according to standard estimates, what percentage of American males are straight?

188
01:28:06,060 --> 01:28:10,060
Higher? Like 98? 98.

189
01:28:10,060 --> 01:28:13,060
All right. And so if, you know,

190
01:28:13,060 --> 01:28:17,260
if even if 100% of all gay men only wear pink all the time

191
01:28:17,260 --> 01:28:23,060
and only one straight man out of 25 were wearing pink on a given day still,

192
01:28:23,060 --> 01:28:29,060
this is going to give you, most of the men in pink will actually be straight.

193
01:28:29,060 --> 01:28:34,260
All right. So, you know, in general, anything that is rare is likely to get overestimated

194
01:28:34,260 --> 01:28:40,260
based upon some very superficial indicators which might be, which even could be correlated.

195
01:28:40,260 --> 01:28:45,460
And yet if the thing is rare to begin with, it doesn't really mean very much.

196
01:28:45,460 --> 01:28:49,060
All right. So questions about that?

197
01:28:49,060 --> 01:28:54,660
Yeah. So, you know, so just consulting base rates is a good way of getting an idea about what's going on.

198
01:28:54,660 --> 01:28:58,260
Now, again, like, people often get resentful of this because base rates often

199
01:28:58,260 --> 01:29:03,060
will lead people to not believe in themselves anymore.

200
01:29:03,060 --> 01:29:04,660
And that's terrible, right?

201
01:29:04,660 --> 01:29:07,660
It would be terrible if people believed, the average person believed they were average.

202
01:29:07,660 --> 01:29:11,260
Wouldn't that be a disaster?

203
01:29:11,260 --> 01:29:17,860
Or maybe it would be, so, because nothing ever comes wrong from believing in yourself too much, right?

204
01:29:17,860 --> 01:29:24,060
Like, if you believe that you'll never die in an accident, that couldn't leave anything bad, could it?

205
01:29:24,060 --> 01:29:26,660
Or could it? Yeah.

206
01:29:26,660 --> 01:29:30,860
Of course, it would be terrible, right? You could die, you could kill people, right?

207
01:29:30,860 --> 01:29:36,060
So you believe that, or you're believing that you're sure to go and finish graduate school.

208
01:29:36,060 --> 01:29:38,860
You might want to look at the base rates. Well, what fraction of the people?

209
01:29:38,860 --> 01:29:42,460
And then, you know, so, now, of course, if you can get additional information,

210
01:29:42,460 --> 01:29:45,060
you might want to go and look at more sophisticated base rates.

211
01:29:45,060 --> 01:29:50,060
So you might say, well, how about the graduation rate of the people at the school where I met?

212
01:29:50,060 --> 01:29:53,860
Or the base rate of people at my GRE school at that?

213
01:29:53,860 --> 01:29:57,260
So, you know, like, there's no reason not to go get additional information.

214
01:29:57,260 --> 01:30:02,460
But still, it's always good to start with what normally happens and then adjust up or down

215
01:30:02,460 --> 01:30:07,460
based on your personal characteristics, rather than just ignore base rates.

216
01:30:07,460 --> 01:30:12,260
Which, again, of course, a lot of people do on the theory that they're an individual, not a statistic.

217
01:30:12,260 --> 01:30:15,460
But, let's see, where do we even get statistics from, again?

218
01:30:15,460 --> 01:30:17,660
Collecting data from individuals.

219
01:30:17,660 --> 01:30:23,060
So, you know, the fact that you're an individual does not mean the statistics don't apply to you.

220
01:30:23,060 --> 01:30:28,460
All right. So, questions about availability or representativeness biases?

221
01:30:28,460 --> 01:30:31,460
In the experiment with the engineer psychologist convention,

222
01:30:31,460 --> 01:30:34,260
you said people give the same answer regardless of the base rates.

223
01:30:34,260 --> 01:30:38,660
Will they give answers larger than 30 in the case where it's 30% engineers?

224
01:30:38,660 --> 01:30:42,660
Let's see. I'm trying to remember if there are any. I don't think so.

225
01:30:42,660 --> 01:30:48,460
So, yeah, I mean, I think that, you know, again, like, this is not one of these experiments where the results are really weird.

226
01:30:48,460 --> 01:30:53,260
But, yeah. Okay.

227
01:30:53,260 --> 01:30:56,460
I mean, actually, it wouldn't be that weird to say that, you know, like, that, you know, like,

228
01:30:56,460 --> 01:31:01,660
if 30% are engineers, then it's more than 30% likely that someone wearing arm-rimmed glasses and a pocket protector is an engineer, right?

229
01:31:01,660 --> 01:31:06,060
That would actually be, if you said 30, then that would mean, that would basically be saying there's no correlation.

230
01:31:06,060 --> 01:31:09,260
So, yeah. You definitely expect them to give something higher than that.

231
01:31:09,260 --> 01:31:15,660
But, again, it's more like saying the odds are 80% regardless of whether 30% of the people are engineers or 70% are engineers.

232
01:31:15,660 --> 01:31:19,860
That's the odd part. All right.

233
01:31:19,860 --> 01:31:22,660
All right. And, yeah. And there's so, let's see.

234
01:31:22,660 --> 01:31:28,260
Let's see. All right.

235
01:31:28,260 --> 01:31:36,660
Yeah, yeah. So, yeah. And, by the way, so on the violation of that one, there the classic experiment involves giving people two scenarios.

236
01:31:36,660 --> 01:31:41,660
One of them is you say, you know, Sally has a lifelong interest in the environment.

237
01:31:41,660 --> 01:31:45,060
She loves hiking and animals and donates to environmental causes.

238
01:31:45,060 --> 01:31:48,860
So, people in both experimental treatments get that same prompt.

239
01:31:48,860 --> 01:31:53,460
And then group A is asked, what is the probability that she will be a banker?

240
01:31:53,460 --> 01:31:55,460
And then, of course, people give a really low number.

241
01:31:55,460 --> 01:32:00,660
And then group two is said, what is the probability that she's a banker active in the active environmental causes?

242
01:32:00,660 --> 01:32:06,460
And they actually give a higher number to a banker active environmental causes than to being a banker.

243
01:32:06,460 --> 01:32:10,360
Which makes very little sense in terms of math.

244
01:32:10,360 --> 01:32:18,460
Because, look, either she'll be a banker active environmental causes or she'll be a banker non-environmental,

245
01:32:18,460 --> 01:32:23,260
not of active environmental causes. And the second thing is going to be a little bit above zero anyway.

246
01:32:23,260 --> 01:32:27,060
So, there's no way that the second probability should be higher than the first probability.

247
01:32:27,060 --> 01:32:31,060
And yet, it seems like people are doing some kind of representativeness heuristic where they're saying,

248
01:32:31,060 --> 01:32:35,060
well, none of that, none of the story fits a banker.

249
01:32:35,060 --> 01:32:39,260
But the story does fit a banker active environmental causes.

250
01:32:39,260 --> 01:32:44,660
And then sort of matching, yeah, I can sort of see how that person could evolve into a banker active environmental causes.

251
01:32:44,660 --> 01:32:47,460
Because there's not that much money in environmentalism. It's hard to get a paying job.

252
01:32:47,460 --> 01:32:50,260
So, there's a lot of people who are bankers. So, you might want to be a banker.

