01:32:50,260 --> 01:32:52,760
But you'll still stay true to your ideals, of course.

254
01:32:52,760 --> 01:32:56,060
And then you've got a whole little biography of this woman.

255
01:32:56,060 --> 01:33:00,660
Whereas the other story, you don't have a biography. And it's also like, hmm.

256
01:33:00,660 --> 01:33:07,860
All right. So, questions, questions. All right. Let us then move on.

257
01:33:07,860 --> 01:33:10,660
All right. So, risk misperceptions.

258
01:33:10,660 --> 01:33:16,660
All right. The basic rational expectations assumption is that actors' risk estimates are on average correct.

259
01:33:16,660 --> 01:33:22,060
There is a large empirical literature that checks to see whether risk estimates are on average correct.

260
01:33:22,060 --> 01:33:24,560
And finds that this is not true.

261
01:33:24,560 --> 01:33:30,460
All right. In particular, low probability events seem poorly estimated by people.

262
01:33:30,460 --> 01:33:33,760
Poorly estimated. Although in a strange way.

263
01:33:33,760 --> 01:33:37,760
Right. Because when you give people events that are objectively low probability,

264
01:33:37,760 --> 01:33:39,660
they tend to do one of two things.

265
01:33:39,660 --> 01:33:52,360
Either set the probability equal to zero, or make it too big by a lot. Too big by a lot. Okay.

266
01:33:52,360 --> 01:33:59,360
So, like for example, what is the probability that a randomly selected American is Jewish?

267
01:33:59,360 --> 01:34:04,760
Well, of course, you know. So, if you just go and ask regular Americans, it's like 20%.

268
01:34:04,760 --> 01:34:08,060
Right. And what's the probability that a randomly selected American is black,

269
01:34:08,060 --> 01:34:11,760
according to the average American? Probably something like 30%.

270
01:34:11,760 --> 01:34:17,160
And then you go, how about Italian? Well, like another 15%. And Greek? Seven. All right.

271
01:34:17,160 --> 01:34:20,960
And pretty soon you've given away the entire 100% probability.

272
01:34:20,960 --> 01:34:26,560
And we haven't even gotten to the Irish, or English, or any of the groups that historically

273
01:34:26,560 --> 01:34:30,660
are actually the largest ethnic groups in the US.

274
01:34:30,660 --> 01:34:37,360
All right. So, that's the case of assigning probabilities that are too big to low probability events.

275
01:34:37,360 --> 01:34:38,360
Yes.

276
01:34:38,360 --> 01:34:41,360
It's going to be Italian and Irish at the same time, so you can overlap.

277
01:34:41,360 --> 01:34:45,560
Yes. So, yes. There is that. So, again, like, you know, so, let's see.

278
01:34:45,560 --> 01:34:53,160
In the case of Jewish, normally, let's see. Would you actually bother phrasing it as Jewish and not another thing?

279
01:34:53,160 --> 01:34:56,660
You probably wouldn't bother. Again, like, so, in practice, when you go and ask people,

280
01:34:56,660 --> 01:35:01,560
the reason why they're giving the numbers is not that they think there's a lot of overlap.

281
01:35:01,560 --> 01:35:06,360
The reason, rather, is that they just, or they don't get, like, overlap is something that, oh,

282
01:35:06,360 --> 01:35:09,960
I mean, you could actually probably make the result even more extreme if you said,

283
01:35:09,960 --> 01:35:12,960
oh, probably somebody's Irish. How about Irish and black?

284
01:35:12,960 --> 01:35:15,360
And then they'll give you another view probability for that.

285
01:35:15,360 --> 01:35:18,360
And so you probably actually get up to 100% even sooner.

286
01:35:18,360 --> 01:35:22,760
Because when they were thinking of the Irish, they were not thinking of mixed groups or blends.

287
01:35:22,760 --> 01:35:27,560
They were thinking of guys with restylepricon outfits.

288
01:35:27,560 --> 01:35:31,560
And that was what they had in their minds.

289
01:35:31,560 --> 01:35:35,160
All right. So, anyway, so a lot of times people assign probabilities that are too big.

290
01:35:35,160 --> 01:35:39,160
And let's see, probability you'll die in a terrorist attack.

291
01:35:39,160 --> 01:35:41,560
Yeah, that's probably a too big one. All right.

292
01:35:41,560 --> 01:35:47,960
Now, on the other hand, there are things where it does seem like a lot of people round down to zero.

293
01:35:47,960 --> 01:35:54,160
They round down to zero. And, again, these are for things where, in fact, the odds are probably pretty good.

294
01:35:54,160 --> 01:35:59,560
Like, how about the odds that you die because your doctor didn't wash his hands?

