329
01:38:33,360 --> 01:38:42,160
where the things that people tend to overestimate are things where it's very vivid or memorable.

330
01:38:42,160 --> 01:38:52,160
So now there may also be an issue when you're doing demographics that people just don't seem to really understand that there's only 100 percent to hand out.

331
01:38:52,160 --> 01:38:59,360
So this was another weird result that Tetlock found, was that if you go and ask people to go and rate,

332
01:38:59,360 --> 01:39:04,960
to assign probabilities to all logically possible events, they generally assign way more possibility.

333
01:39:04,960 --> 01:39:12,760
And especially the more you subdivide the events into more detailed events, the more likely each scenario becomes.

334
01:39:12,760 --> 01:39:19,960
And again, of course, logically, if you say, what are the odds that there's a terror attack carried out on a Monday by a guy named Fred

335
01:39:19,960 --> 01:39:29,360
wearing a jumpsuit with tie-dye on it, right, you'd have to, like logically, that would have to be an astronomically low probability, right?

336
01:39:29,360 --> 01:39:32,560
But again, a lot of people want to say, eh, I don't know, like one in a thousand,

337
01:39:32,560 --> 01:39:38,160
never realizing that when you split it up into that many scenarios, you could have hundreds of thousands of scenarios.

338
01:39:38,160 --> 01:39:43,360
And then if you have 100,000 scenarios, a one in a thousand chance each,

339
01:39:43,360 --> 01:39:48,560
pretty soon you've got a massive abundance of live scenarios.

340
01:39:48,560 --> 01:39:53,360
By the way, this was the one way in which Tetlock found out that really dogmatic, single-theory,

341
01:39:53,360 --> 01:39:56,760
explain-everything people are more accurate than open-minded people,

342
01:39:56,760 --> 01:40:20,760
because open-minded people hand out probability like it's candy.


