01:50:36,000 --> 01:50:39,000
So then it's easy to go and act like it's a great thing,

227
01:50:39,000 --> 01:50:42,000
even though no one knows what's great about it.

228
01:50:42,000 --> 01:50:46,000
Okay. All right. Questions, questions?

229
01:50:46,000 --> 01:50:50,000
All right. So, yes.

230
01:50:50,000 --> 01:50:52,000
None of my kids would mount climbers.

231
01:50:52,000 --> 01:50:55,000
Not allowed.

232
01:50:55,000 --> 01:50:58,000
All right. So, the last topic here.

233
01:50:58,000 --> 01:51:00,000
So systematically biased beliefs about economics.

234
01:51:00,000 --> 01:51:03,000
We talked about this before, but now we will come back to it

235
01:51:03,000 --> 01:51:05,000
in more detail.

236
01:51:05,000 --> 01:51:07,000
All right. So go back to your intro economics class.

237
01:51:07,000 --> 01:51:10,000
Since you're here, I'm guessing you had a pretty good one.

238
01:51:10,000 --> 01:51:13,000
Maybe not, but I'm assuming you probably had a pretty good intro econ class.

239
01:51:13,000 --> 01:51:16,000
And if yours was anything like mine, what was exciting about it

240
01:51:16,000 --> 01:51:20,000
is that you had a professor who was saying that

241
01:51:20,000 --> 01:51:22,000
most of the world is wrong.

242
01:51:22,000 --> 01:51:25,000
And not just randomly wrong, but wrong in a systematic way.

243
01:51:25,000 --> 01:51:27,000
And who are most of the people?

244
01:51:27,000 --> 01:51:30,000
Well, your parents, your teachers,

245
01:51:30,000 --> 01:51:34,000
the textbook authors that you read in elementary school

246
01:51:34,000 --> 01:51:36,000
through high school. All wrong.

247
01:51:36,000 --> 01:51:38,000
All important stuff they didn't understand.

248
01:51:38,000 --> 01:51:40,000
All right.

249
01:51:40,000 --> 01:51:43,000
So, you know, if they know everything like why free trade is good.

250
01:51:43,000 --> 01:51:45,000
So, economists teach us comparative advantage.

251
01:51:45,000 --> 01:51:47,000
And they expect students to say,

252
01:51:47,000 --> 01:51:50,000
Oh, so, comparative, like, so, like, like.

253
01:51:50,000 --> 01:51:53,000
So free trade is just as good as I thought it was.

254
01:51:53,000 --> 01:51:55,000
No better, no worse.

255
01:51:55,000 --> 01:51:57,000
Now, when you teach comparative advantage,

256
01:51:57,000 --> 01:51:59,000
you are normally thinking that students will change their minds

257
01:51:59,000 --> 01:52:01,000
in what direction?

258
01:52:01,000 --> 01:52:03,000
A pro-trade.

259
01:52:03,000 --> 01:52:06,000
Right, because you'll say, you probably think that trade is only beneficial

260
01:52:06,000 --> 01:52:09,000
if one country is an absolute advantage in one thing

261
01:52:09,000 --> 01:52:11,000
and another is an absolute advantage in another.

262
01:52:11,000 --> 01:52:13,000
I'm going to prove that there's mutually beneficial trade

263
01:52:13,000 --> 01:52:16,000
even if one country is an absolute advantage in everything.

264
01:52:16,000 --> 01:52:18,000
Right, and meanwhile your students are like,

265
01:52:18,000 --> 01:52:21,000
No, no, no, I just think trade is bad, always.

266
01:52:21,000 --> 01:52:23,000
I think trade is terrible.

267
01:52:23,000 --> 01:52:25,000
And it's the way other countries rip us off.

268
01:52:25,000 --> 01:52:28,000
But anyway, comparative advantage is a lot more optimistic than that.

269
01:52:28,000 --> 01:52:29,000
Right.

270
01:52:29,000 --> 01:52:32,000
Or again, when economists go and teach students about price controls,

271
01:52:32,000 --> 01:52:34,000
are they thinking the students are going to be like,

272
01:52:34,000 --> 01:52:36,000
Oh, okay, so half the students will say,

273
01:52:36,000 --> 01:52:38,000
Oh, so price controls are better than I thought

274
01:52:38,000 --> 01:52:40,000
and half will think it's worse than they thought.

275
01:52:40,000 --> 01:52:43,000
No, they are thinking the students will then,

276
01:52:43,000 --> 01:52:45,000
will previously have been in favor of price controls

277
01:52:45,000 --> 01:52:48,000
but afterwards will be against.

278
01:52:48,000 --> 01:52:49,000
Right.

279
01:52:49,000 --> 01:52:53,000
So I mean, I remember when I first heard the basic economics of the minimum wage.

280
01:52:53,000 --> 01:52:55,000
And my honest reaction was,

281
01:52:55,000 --> 01:52:59,000
in my entire life I have never once heard a single person

282
01:52:59,000 --> 01:53:02,000
say anything bad about the minimum wage ever.

283
01:53:02,000 --> 01:53:04,000
It's not that I previously heard someone say,

284
01:53:04,000 --> 01:53:07,000
Well, of course we all know that there is a potential for a disemployment effect,

285
01:53:07,000 --> 01:53:10,000
but a peer of less would say that it's small.

286
01:53:10,000 --> 01:53:12,000
I never heard anyone say this.

287
01:53:12,000 --> 01:53:15,000
And I did not grow up in a left wing enclave like Berkeley.

288
01:53:15,000 --> 01:53:18,000
I grew up in a totally normal Los Angeles suburb.

289
01:53:18,000 --> 01:53:20,000
And I knew plenty of Republicans,

290
01:53:20,000 --> 01:53:23,000
but I didn't know a single Republican was against the minimum wage.

291
01:53:23,000 --> 01:53:24,000
Right.

292
01:53:24,000 --> 01:53:26,000
And then I said, Wow, there's economists saying,

293
01:53:26,000 --> 01:53:28,000
You know the thing that everyone thinks is good?

294
01:53:28,000 --> 01:53:30,000
Maybe it's terrible.

295
01:53:30,000 --> 01:53:34,000
And the only way to know is to go and do some complex measurements.

296
01:53:34,000 --> 01:53:36,000
This would be like saying,

297
01:53:36,000 --> 01:53:39,000
Maybe apple pie is actually really bad.

298
01:53:39,000 --> 01:53:42,000
And we need to do some measurements to find out if it is or not.

299
01:53:42,000 --> 01:53:43,000
Right.

300
01:53:43,000 --> 01:53:46,000
And everyone's been telling you apple pie is wonderful your whole life.

301
01:53:46,000 --> 01:53:47,000
Right.

302
01:53:47,000 --> 01:53:48,000
And so anyway,

303
01:53:48,000 --> 01:53:50,000
this is the way inter-recon is often taught.

304
01:53:50,000 --> 01:53:53,000
And a lot of famous historical economists operate from the same perspective.

305
01:53:53,000 --> 01:53:55,000
So Adam Smith.

306
01:53:55,000 --> 01:53:57,000
So much of the book is written assuming that

307
01:53:57,000 --> 01:54:00,000
the reader will start off as a protectionist

308
01:54:00,000 --> 01:54:02,000
and a proponent of a government.

309
01:54:02,000 --> 01:54:03,000
And after reading it,

310
01:54:03,000 --> 01:54:06,000
they will realize how naive they were.

311
01:54:06,000 --> 01:54:09,000
And how arrogant they were in thinking that they or anyone would know enough

312
01:54:09,000 --> 01:54:12,000
to plan the world for the better.

313
01:54:12,000 --> 01:54:15,000
We can see this in Frederick Bastiat.

314
01:54:15,000 --> 01:54:17,000
Or you know, you can see it in Frank Knight.

315
01:54:17,000 --> 01:54:19,000
Or Milton Friedman.

316
01:54:19,000 --> 01:54:20,000
Or me.

317
01:54:20,000 --> 01:54:21,000
Right.

318
01:54:21,000 --> 01:54:23,000
So you can see, so this is, you know,

319
01:54:23,000 --> 01:54:24,000
so economists throughout history,

320
01:54:24,000 --> 01:54:25,000
there's a general sense,

321
01:54:25,000 --> 01:54:27,000
or like Paul Krugman.

322
01:54:27,000 --> 01:54:28,000
You can see this in Paul Krugman.

323
01:54:28,000 --> 01:54:33,000
Does Paul Krugman assume that people basically have the right view about free trade

324
01:54:33,000 --> 01:54:37,000
and that he's just there to go and approve the accuracy of the details?

325
01:54:37,000 --> 01:54:38,000
No.

326
01:54:38,000 --> 01:54:40,000
I mean like actually, you know,

327
01:54:40,000 --> 01:54:45,000
so Krugman has some early work on strategic trade theory.

328
01:54:45,000 --> 01:54:49,000
Giving high tech cases where free trade might not be a good idea.

329
01:54:49,000 --> 01:54:51,000
But then he actually has a piece saying,

330
01:54:51,000 --> 01:54:52,000
what should we teach to undergraduates?

331
01:54:52,000 --> 01:54:54,000
And he said, don't teach them that stuff.

332
01:54:54,000 --> 01:54:56,000
They don't need to hear that stuff.

333
01:54:56,000 --> 01:54:57,000
Why not?

334
01:54:57,000 --> 01:54:59,000
Because they don't even know the basics yet.

335
01:54:59,000 --> 01:55:02,000
They need to know what Hume and Ricardo said.

336
01:55:02,000 --> 01:55:04,000
And if they can learn that,

337
01:55:04,000 --> 01:55:06,000
and after they really understand that,

338
01:55:06,000 --> 01:55:07,000
and if they will come and say,

339
01:55:07,000 --> 01:55:08,000
can you tell me more,

340
01:55:08,000 --> 01:55:09,000
then maybe we'll teach them my stuff.

341
01:55:09,000 --> 01:55:10,000
Maybe.

342
01:55:10,000 --> 01:55:13,000
But what people really need are just the very basics,

343
01:55:13,000 --> 01:55:14,000
the very basic stuff,

344
01:55:14,000 --> 01:55:17,000
which Krugman thought very few people actually have.

345
01:55:17,000 --> 01:55:19,000
And if you don't have,

346
01:55:19,000 --> 01:55:20,000
and then if you don't have,

347
01:55:20,000 --> 01:55:22,000
if you don't know the basic stuff of Hume and Ricardo,

348
01:55:22,000 --> 01:55:23,000
what are you?

349
01:55:23,000 --> 01:55:25,000
You're like a strategic trade theorist.

350
01:55:25,000 --> 01:55:26,000
You're someone saying,

351
01:55:26,000 --> 01:55:29,000
how can we figure out a way to import nothing

352
01:55:29,000 --> 01:55:31,000
and export a ton of stuff?

353
01:55:31,000 --> 01:55:34,000
Or maybe you're even worse guys.

354
01:55:34,000 --> 01:55:36,000
How can we figure out a way to get rid of the trade deficit

355
01:55:36,000 --> 01:55:38,000
and have lots of foreign investment here?

356
01:55:38,000 --> 01:55:41,000
And yes, if you know the accounting,

357
01:55:41,000 --> 01:55:43,000
that is just logically impossible

358
01:55:43,000 --> 01:55:47,000
because foreign investment automatically increases

359
01:55:47,000 --> 01:55:49,000
the current account deficit.

360
01:55:49,000 --> 01:55:52,000
Again, what does it mean to have a current account deficit?

361
01:55:52,000 --> 01:55:55,000
It means that you are paying for goods with assets.

362
01:55:55,000 --> 01:55:58,000
And that is investment in our country,

363
01:55:58,000 --> 01:56:01,000
is when we are going and selling people

364
01:56:01,000 --> 01:56:02,000
in other countries' assets.

365
01:56:02,000 --> 01:56:03,000
When they invest here,

366
01:56:03,000 --> 01:56:06,000
how does a company go and invest in a factory here?

367
01:56:06,000 --> 01:56:11,000
They come here and they go and they buy some of our assets.

368
01:56:11,000 --> 01:56:14,000
They buy a factory.

369
01:56:14,000 --> 01:56:15,000
All right.

370
01:56:15,000 --> 01:56:17,000
So this is lurking in the background

371
01:56:17,000 --> 01:56:19,000
of a whole tradition in economics

372
01:56:19,000 --> 01:56:22,000
of thinking of the economic beliefs of the public

373
01:56:22,000 --> 01:56:24,000
as being systematically biased.

374
01:56:24,000 --> 01:56:28,000
And yet, when economists actually model

375
01:56:28,000 --> 01:56:30,000
what people's economic beliefs are,

376
01:56:30,000 --> 01:56:33,000
they almost always assume rational expectations.

377
01:56:33,000 --> 01:56:34,000
So when economists write down a model

378
01:56:34,000 --> 01:56:37,000
of determination of tariff rates,

379
01:56:37,000 --> 01:56:40,000
normally they'll come up with some model

380
01:56:40,000 --> 01:56:41,000
where you've got,

381
01:56:41,000 --> 01:56:44,000
there's a conflict between interest groups and consumers,

382
01:56:44,000 --> 01:56:48,000
but each group is assumed to know accurately,

383
01:56:48,000 --> 01:56:52,000
on average, what is the best tariff rate for that group.

384
01:56:52,000 --> 01:56:54,000
So in these models,

385
01:56:54,000 --> 01:56:55,000
they almost never have consumers

386
01:56:55,000 --> 01:56:58,000
falsely believe that tariffs are good,

387
01:56:58,000 --> 01:57:00,000
and then we get tariffs which are inefficient.

388
01:57:00,000 --> 01:57:03,000
Which again, sounds kind of trivial for a model, right?

389
01:57:03,000 --> 01:57:05,000
But the fact that it's a trivial model

390
01:57:05,000 --> 01:57:07,000
doesn't mean that it's a wrong model.

391
01:57:07,000 --> 01:57:08,000
It just means that there's no jobs

392
01:57:08,000 --> 01:57:12,000
for economists to be had in publishing on it.

393
01:57:12,000 --> 01:57:13,000
Okay.

394
01:57:13,000 --> 01:57:14,000
All right.

395
01:57:14,000 --> 01:57:17,000
Now, so I've got a series of empirical papers

396
01:57:17,000 --> 01:57:18,000
where I try to see

397
01:57:18,000 --> 01:57:21,000
who's actually right about the public's economic beliefs.

398
01:57:21,000 --> 01:57:23,000
Is it the textbooks

399
01:57:23,000 --> 01:57:26,000
and the greatness of economists throughout history?

400
01:57:26,000 --> 01:57:31,000
Or is it the current theoretical models?

401
01:57:31,000 --> 01:57:33,000
So I've got papers still here to test,

402
01:57:33,000 --> 01:57:36,000
and the results overwhelmingly come out in favor

403
01:57:36,000 --> 01:57:40,000
of textbooks and the historical economists,

404
01:57:40,000 --> 01:57:44,000
and overwhelmingly against people going

405
01:57:44,000 --> 01:57:49,000
and writing rational expectations models of voter reliefs.

406
01:57:49,000 --> 01:57:50,000
Okay.

407
01:57:50,000 --> 01:57:51,000
All right, so the data is,

408
01:57:51,000 --> 01:57:53,000
this was constructed by someone other than me,

409
01:57:53,000 --> 01:57:55,000
which as I said is great for two reasons.

410
01:57:55,000 --> 01:57:57,000
First of all,

411
01:57:57,000 --> 01:58:02,000
then the reputation of the data is not tainted

412
01:58:02,000 --> 01:58:05,000
by the fact that I'm the researcher doing it.

413
01:58:05,000 --> 01:58:07,000
And I will admit this.

414
01:58:07,000 --> 01:58:10,000
Am I the kind of person who believed in the textbook view

415
01:58:10,000 --> 01:58:11,000
more than the research view from the outset?

416
01:58:11,000 --> 01:58:12,000
Yes, I am.

417
01:58:12,000 --> 01:58:14,000
Which means that it's better to outsource the data collection

418
01:58:14,000 --> 01:58:17,000
to someone who doesn't have a dog in the fight.

419
01:58:17,000 --> 01:58:18,000
And of course, also,

420
01:58:18,000 --> 01:58:20,000
they paid for all the data collection,

421
01:58:20,000 --> 01:58:22,000
which today might be half a million dollars

422
01:58:22,000 --> 01:58:23,000
to collect this data set,

423
01:58:23,000 --> 01:58:24,000
and it cost me nothing.

424
01:58:24,000 --> 01:58:26,000
They just gave it to me for free.

425
01:58:26,000 --> 01:58:28,000
And I think last week I was mentioning

426
01:58:28,000 --> 01:58:30,000
how there's a lot of data that exists

427
01:58:30,000 --> 01:58:32,000
and that is on the internet and has never been used.

428
01:58:32,000 --> 01:58:35,000
There's also a lot of data that is not on the internet,

429
01:58:35,000 --> 01:58:37,000
but has barely been used,

430
01:58:37,000 --> 01:58:38,000
and if you just go and email the people

431
01:58:38,000 --> 01:58:41,000
who did the study, they'll give it to you.

432
01:58:41,000 --> 01:58:43,000
So I probably got lucky.

433
01:58:43,000 --> 01:58:46,000
If you email four different people with data sets

434
01:58:46,000 --> 01:58:48,000
and they're done with them,

435
01:58:48,000 --> 01:58:50,000
and they say, you know, can I have it?

436
01:58:50,000 --> 01:58:53,000
I think you'll probably get one yes.

437
01:58:53,000 --> 01:58:56,000
So in general, if you just keep your eyes open

438
01:58:56,000 --> 01:58:59,000
for data sets that seem to be rarely used.

439
01:58:59,000 --> 01:59:00,000
Now again, if you wait too long,

440
01:59:00,000 --> 01:59:02,000
then they'll just give you the dog ate my data story.

441
01:59:02,000 --> 01:59:05,000
Like, well, sorry, I haven't had that data in years.

442
01:59:05,000 --> 01:59:06,000
Yeah, too bad it cost half a million dollars

443
01:59:06,000 --> 01:59:08,000
and I only got one paper out of it.

444
01:59:08,000 --> 01:59:10,000
Would have been nice if someone else could have used it

445
01:59:10,000 --> 01:59:14,000
and learned more, but there's no way of saving data.

446
01:59:14,000 --> 01:59:15,000
Unfortunately.

447
01:59:15,000 --> 01:59:17,000
Too bad.

448
01:59:17,000 --> 01:59:19,000
Anyway, so basically you want to give them enough time

449
01:59:19,000 --> 01:59:21,000
where they've sucked it dry,

450
01:59:21,000 --> 01:59:25,000
but not so much time that they've thrown it away.

451
01:59:25,000 --> 01:59:28,000
And if they've sucked it dry, what can you do with it?

452
01:59:28,000 --> 01:59:29,000
Often a lot, because a lot of people

453
01:59:29,000 --> 01:59:31,000
don't have your creativity.

454
01:59:31,000 --> 01:59:32,000
Do they?

455
01:59:32,000 --> 01:59:33,000
No.

456
01:59:33,000 --> 01:59:34,000
The answer is no.

457
01:59:34,000 --> 01:59:38,000
Other people do not have your creativity,

458
01:59:38,000 --> 01:59:40,000
and so you will see uses for their data

459
01:59:40,000 --> 01:59:42,000
that they're not aware of.

460
01:59:42,000 --> 01:59:45,000
Okay, so the method that I used in here

461
01:59:45,000 --> 01:59:47,000
was estimating beliefs as a function of

462
01:59:47,000 --> 01:59:49,000
DECOM, DEMI, and control variables.

463
01:59:49,000 --> 01:59:50,000
So you guys remember DEMI?

464
01:59:50,000 --> 01:59:51,000
So DEMI variable for economists,

465
01:59:51,000 --> 01:59:54,000
one if you're an economist, zero otherwise.

466
01:59:54,000 --> 01:59:55,000
All right?

467
01:59:55,000 --> 01:59:57,000
And again, if you've got rationalizations,

468
01:59:57,000 --> 01:60:09,000
then we go and estimate the belief as a function of A.


1
01:60:00,000 --> 01:60:03,160
plus B times the dummy variable, plus, say,

2
01:60:03,160 --> 01:60:06,240
gamma times the vector of controls.

3
01:60:06,240 --> 01:60:09,440
And rational estimation says that B will be equal to 0.

4
01:60:09,440 --> 01:60:11,560
There will be no difference in the average opinion

5
01:60:11,560 --> 01:60:13,160
of experts in the public, right?

6
01:60:13,160 --> 01:60:15,200
Because in the rational estimation's world,

7
01:60:15,200 --> 01:60:17,480
what is the effect of knowledge?

8
01:60:17,480 --> 01:60:19,680
Is it to shift means?

9
01:60:19,680 --> 01:60:21,920
No, it's to compress variance.

10
01:60:21,920 --> 01:60:25,120
So if that's the truth, capital T star,

11
01:60:25,120 --> 01:60:28,000
then ignorant people have that distribution,

12
01:60:28,000 --> 01:60:29,800
and informed people have that distribution.

13
01:60:29,800 --> 01:60:34,760
That's what rational estimations is all about.

14
01:60:34,760 --> 01:60:36,280
Or again, so why the controls?

15
01:60:36,280 --> 01:60:38,040
The controls are there just because there's

16
01:60:38,040 --> 01:60:40,160
a lot of people who think that economics

17
01:60:40,160 --> 01:60:43,080
is a fake discipline, or at least a biased discipline.

18
01:60:43,080 --> 01:60:47,760
And so the controls are there to deal with the objections.

19
01:60:47,760 --> 01:60:49,680
And there are two main reasons people

20
01:60:49,680 --> 01:60:52,480
look for for mistrusting economists.

21
01:60:52,480 --> 01:60:56,160
So one is that economists have some kind

22
01:60:56,160 --> 01:60:57,160
of self-serving bias.

23
01:60:57,160 --> 01:60:59,000
So maybe it just means that if they're rich

24
01:60:59,000 --> 01:61:00,520
they got on job security, so they're

25
01:61:00,520 --> 01:61:02,320
out of touch with the American people,

26
01:61:02,320 --> 01:61:04,560
out of touch with the problems of the American people.

27
01:61:04,560 --> 01:61:06,920
So of course they think that international trade

28
01:61:06,920 --> 01:61:08,880
or downsizing are good when they personally

29
01:61:08,880 --> 01:61:12,240
are immune to the bad part, as well, the bad stuff.

30
01:61:12,240 --> 01:61:14,920
So that is a story, but it's a testable story.

31
01:61:14,920 --> 01:61:16,600
Because you can get measures of whatever

32
01:61:16,600 --> 01:61:19,360
it is that supposedly is biasing economists' judgment,

33
01:61:19,360 --> 01:61:21,720
and then we put that control variable in.

34
01:61:21,720 --> 01:61:25,320
And when you do that, that is like going and pairing

35
01:61:25,320 --> 01:61:28,520
an economist with high income and job security

36
01:61:28,520 --> 01:61:32,120
to a non-economist with high income and job security.

37
01:61:32,120 --> 01:61:36,040
Or alternately, it's like comparing a normal person

38
01:61:36,040 --> 01:61:37,600
with someone with economic training,

39
01:61:37,600 --> 01:61:40,160
but all the problems of a normal person.

40
01:61:40,160 --> 01:61:42,560
That's the whole idea of putting in the control variables.

41
01:61:42,560 --> 01:61:44,080
And again, if this theory is right,

42
01:61:44,080 --> 01:61:46,480
it is driven by personal circumstances,

43
01:61:46,480 --> 01:61:49,160
then that should make the gap go away.

44
01:61:49,160 --> 01:61:52,720
But in practice, it almost never does.

45
01:61:52,720 --> 01:61:55,260
And then another story that's been told of ideological bias,

46
01:61:55,260 --> 01:61:56,800
this one just says that economics

47
01:61:56,800 --> 01:62:00,240
is a coven of extreme right-wing ideology.

48
01:62:00,240 --> 01:62:02,880
And people believe it because if you don't believe it,

49
01:62:02,880 --> 01:62:04,320
you don't go into the discipline.

50
01:62:04,320 --> 01:62:06,200
So of course, of course economists

51
01:62:06,200 --> 01:62:08,240
believe this horrible stuff.

52
01:62:08,240 --> 01:62:10,640
And again, this is something where you go and get measures

53
01:62:10,640 --> 01:62:13,720
of people's ideology or party, and then

54
01:62:13,720 --> 01:62:15,880
try to see whether that explains the difference.

55
01:62:15,880 --> 01:62:18,920
So if you are a very liberal democratic economist,

56
01:62:18,920 --> 01:62:20,520
do you then believe the same stuff

57
01:62:20,520 --> 01:62:23,880
that very liberal democratic non-economists believe?

58
01:62:23,880 --> 01:62:26,440
And again, the answer is totally not.

59
01:62:26,440 --> 01:62:28,640
Paul Krugman's beliefs are very different from that

60
01:62:28,640 --> 01:62:32,200
of the people that he claims to speak for.

61
01:62:32,200 --> 01:62:34,680
And one of his main goals, especially in earlier years,

62
01:62:34,680 --> 01:62:38,000
was to try to fix other people on his side

63
01:62:38,000 --> 01:62:41,880
and introduce them to economics.

64
01:62:41,880 --> 01:62:44,840
So that is an ideal.

65
01:62:44,840 --> 01:62:46,440
People often have the idea, well, look,

66
01:62:46,440 --> 01:62:47,640
all this free market stuff, that's

67
01:62:47,640 --> 01:62:48,760
just the right-wing people.

68
01:62:48,760 --> 01:62:50,680
It's like, well, no, actually, a lot of this

69
01:62:50,680 --> 01:62:53,440
is stuff that almost all economists believe.

70
01:62:53,440 --> 01:62:55,480
Right now, a safer this is just to say, well,

71
01:62:55,480 --> 01:62:57,720
economists are right-wing regardless of what they say.

72
01:63:00,200 --> 01:63:04,400
So Paul Krugman's right-wing, absolutely.

73
01:63:04,400 --> 01:63:07,960
If you read a book like, what is it?

74
01:63:07,960 --> 01:63:10,520
It's Naomi Klein, Shock, Soccer, and Not Nanny Wolfe.

75
01:63:10,520 --> 01:63:12,360
So I'm just reading a section where

76
01:63:12,360 --> 01:63:14,480
so her take on South Africa.

77
01:63:14,480 --> 01:63:17,400
Her take on South Africa is another case

78
01:63:17,400 --> 01:63:20,760
where neoliberal Chicago boys won the country

79
01:63:20,760 --> 01:63:22,080
and got everything they wanted.

80
01:63:22,080 --> 01:63:26,880
So what are you talking about?

81
01:63:26,880 --> 01:63:31,440
But if you're someone who thinks that the slightest concession

82
01:63:31,440 --> 01:63:33,680
to not having a communist revolution

83
01:63:33,680 --> 01:63:35,920
shows that Milton Friedman runs your country,

84
01:63:35,920 --> 01:63:37,960
then, of course, you're going to see Milton

85
01:63:37,960 --> 01:63:39,440
Friedman ruling the world.

86
01:63:39,440 --> 01:63:41,360
Look, Milton Friedman rules every country

87
01:63:41,360 --> 01:63:44,840
except North Korea and Cuba and Venezuela.

88
01:63:44,840 --> 01:63:49,480
Because they're the only countries that

89
01:63:49,480 --> 01:63:52,600
are doing exactly the opposite of what he recommends.

90
01:63:52,600 --> 01:63:54,880
And everybody else is listening to him.

91
01:63:54,880 --> 01:63:56,440
Wouldn't it be great if you could just

92
01:63:56,440 --> 01:63:58,240
tell your parents this when they say you don't listen to them

93
01:63:58,240 --> 01:63:59,080
and you say, no.

94
01:63:59,080 --> 01:64:01,040
I mean, I've listened to you like three times,

95
01:64:01,040 --> 01:64:03,640
so you rule my life.

96
01:64:03,640 --> 01:64:08,640
So not very compelling, but still.

97
01:64:08,640 --> 01:64:11,040
Now, the big clusters of error.

98
01:64:11,040 --> 01:64:13,200
So by the way, if you don't want to read the book,

99
01:64:13,200 --> 01:64:16,320
you can just go and Google the four cartoons that

100
01:64:16,320 --> 01:64:17,840
have been made about the book.

101
01:64:17,840 --> 01:64:20,720
So there are four cartoons, one on each of these biases.

102
01:64:20,720 --> 01:64:22,520
So you can just watch the cartoon.

103
01:64:22,520 --> 01:64:24,080
And they're a very well-done cartoon.

104
01:64:24,080 --> 01:64:25,320
It's very happy.

105
01:64:25,320 --> 01:64:28,720
I'm very flatteringly drawn.

106
01:64:28,720 --> 01:64:30,960
Thinner, younger, better dressed than I've

107
01:64:30,960 --> 01:64:33,160
ever seen in real life.

108
01:64:33,160 --> 01:64:38,120
But so here are the big clusters that I talk about in the book.

109
01:64:38,120 --> 01:64:40,360
The first one I call anti-market bias.

110
01:64:40,360 --> 01:64:43,920
So there's a general tendency for non-economists

111
01:64:43,920 --> 01:64:46,000
to underestimate the social benefits of the market

112
01:64:46,000 --> 01:64:47,400
mechanism.

113
01:64:47,400 --> 01:64:48,920
This does not mean that all economists

114
01:64:48,920 --> 01:64:51,080
think markets are always great or anything like that.

115
01:64:51,080 --> 01:64:52,000
That's totally wrong.

116
01:64:52,000 --> 01:64:53,760
But still, there's a general tendency

117
01:64:53,760 --> 01:64:55,880
for economists to be more optimistic about markets

118
01:64:55,880 --> 01:64:58,240
to the public, because the public generally

119
01:64:58,240 --> 01:65:01,440
looks to see whether someone is trying to make money.

120
01:65:01,440 --> 01:65:05,680
And if that is so, lead to a negative inference.

121
01:65:05,680 --> 01:65:08,400
On the other hand, economists have almost the opposite

122
01:65:08,400 --> 01:65:10,000
heuristic.

123
01:65:10,000 --> 01:65:12,520
For economists, the main concern is to say,

124
01:65:12,520 --> 01:65:14,520
well, is there a way for someone to make money

125
01:65:14,520 --> 01:65:15,960
solving this problem?

126
01:65:15,960 --> 01:65:17,600
The answer is yes, but economists tend not

127
01:65:17,600 --> 01:65:18,720
to worry about the problem.

128
01:65:18,720 --> 01:65:20,320
Economists worry about problems where

129
01:65:20,320 --> 01:65:22,040
they cannot think of a way for someone

130
01:65:22,040 --> 01:65:25,040
to profit from a solution.

131
01:65:25,040 --> 01:65:26,920
So economists can't say, well, I can't think of any way

132
01:65:26,920 --> 01:65:28,680
to make money by cleaning up the air.

133
01:65:28,680 --> 01:65:30,440
So that's a problem.

134
01:65:30,440 --> 01:65:32,400
But if you can say, well, I can think

135
01:65:32,400 --> 01:65:34,640
of a way for someone to make money by increasing

136
01:65:34,640 --> 01:65:36,680
the number of available kidneys, well,

137
01:65:36,680 --> 01:65:40,000
then why don't we just have a free market of kidneys?

138
01:65:40,000 --> 01:65:41,600
This is one, unless you'd ever tell you

139
01:65:41,600 --> 01:65:43,280
about my dinner with a Marxist economist

140
01:65:43,280 --> 01:65:45,920
where we talked about kidney donation.

141
01:65:45,920 --> 01:65:47,880
Yes, if you don't remember, so this

142
01:65:47,880 --> 01:65:49,640
was at the New School for Social Research.

143
01:65:49,640 --> 01:65:51,480
This is literally an economics department

144
01:65:51,480 --> 01:65:55,760
with a six-foot-tall poster of Karl Marx in the public area.

145
01:65:55,760 --> 01:65:58,280
It's not just one professor has Marx in his office.

146
01:65:58,280 --> 01:66:00,000
It's like, by the water cooler, there's

147
01:66:00,000 --> 01:66:02,640
six-foot-tall Karl Marx.

148
01:66:02,640 --> 01:66:05,600
Just the face is six feet tall.

149
01:66:05,600 --> 01:66:07,000
And yet, when I had him at dinner,

150
01:66:07,000 --> 01:66:09,400
this is a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity.

151
01:66:09,400 --> 01:66:11,000
So what do you guys think about having

152
01:66:11,000 --> 01:66:12,640
a free market of kidneys?

153
01:66:12,640 --> 01:66:15,200
And the graduate students were like, well, I can definitely

154
01:66:15,200 --> 01:66:16,960
see the argument, but I'm a little worried

155
01:66:16,960 --> 01:66:19,040
that Bohr might not be able to get kidneys.

156
01:66:19,040 --> 01:66:21,680
And the Marxist professor said, no, you have to do this.

157
01:66:21,680 --> 01:66:24,440
It'll save 10,000 lives a year.

158
01:66:24,440 --> 01:66:25,200
Wow.

159
01:66:25,200 --> 01:66:28,600
So here's a table of avowed Marxists

160
01:66:28,600 --> 01:66:31,640
who are more in favor of a free market of human kidneys

161
01:66:31,640 --> 01:66:36,440
than almost any conservative Republican in the country.

162
01:66:36,440 --> 01:66:38,520
And what's the difference?

163
01:66:38,520 --> 01:66:40,760
Economics training is the difference,

164
01:66:40,760 --> 01:66:43,920
because they don't just think of prices as redistributive.

165
01:66:43,920 --> 01:66:47,000
They think of them as affecting the quantity supplied.

166
01:66:50,440 --> 01:66:53,240
So anti-market bias.

167
01:66:53,240 --> 01:66:54,360
Then anti-foreign bias.

168
01:66:54,360 --> 01:66:55,960
So it's one thing to convince Americans

169
01:66:55,960 --> 01:66:57,160
that when one American buys something

170
01:66:57,160 --> 01:66:59,120
from another American, they're both better off.

171
01:66:59,120 --> 01:67:00,480
All right, fine, maybe.

172
01:67:00,480 --> 01:67:02,400
But what if an American is buying something

173
01:67:02,400 --> 01:67:03,840
from a Chinese?

174
01:67:03,840 --> 01:67:08,600
Then, well, we all know what the purpose of this is.

175
01:67:08,600 --> 01:67:13,160
It's for the Chinese to trick us, to rip us off,

176
01:67:13,160 --> 01:67:14,400
to take advantage of us.

177
01:67:14,400 --> 01:67:15,840
That is the whole point.

178
01:67:15,840 --> 01:67:18,960
Sure, we get cheap toys, but they get something much better.

179
01:67:18,960 --> 01:67:20,440
Green piece of paper.

180
01:67:20,440 --> 01:67:22,680
So that's much more orgies.

181
01:67:22,680 --> 01:67:25,520
So that is, again, the underlying idea.

182
01:67:28,480 --> 01:67:32,000
So in the book, I go and point out

183
01:67:32,000 --> 01:67:33,920
that there is a thought experiment

184
01:67:33,920 --> 01:67:36,160
that economists have been using for quite a while just

185
01:67:36,160 --> 01:67:37,800
to illustrate the economist view of trade.

186
01:67:37,800 --> 01:67:41,080
And this is very nicely done in the cartoon.

187
01:67:41,080 --> 01:67:45,120
But the thought experiment is the Iowa car crop.

188
01:67:45,120 --> 01:67:47,200
Actually got a slightly annoyed farmer who says,

189
01:67:47,200 --> 01:67:49,120
Iowa does not grow corn, dummy.

190
01:67:49,120 --> 01:67:50,880
All right, fine.

191
01:67:50,880 --> 01:67:52,680
It's too late to change.

192
01:67:52,680 --> 01:67:55,000
Whatever Iowa grows, I don't know.

193
01:67:55,000 --> 01:67:58,680
Let's just define corn in the 19th century sense.

194
01:67:58,680 --> 01:67:59,880
What you say does grow corn?

195
01:67:59,880 --> 01:68:01,800
I'm from Iowa, so I was surrounded by corn.

196
01:68:01,800 --> 01:68:02,480
So I don't know.

197
01:68:02,480 --> 01:68:04,480
Yeah, so he's right all along.

198
01:68:04,480 --> 01:68:06,880
He pointed me to some statistics on how little corn

199
01:68:06,880 --> 01:68:08,440
is grown in Iowa.

200
01:68:08,440 --> 01:68:10,880
Anyway, the thought experiment says this.

201
01:68:10,880 --> 01:68:13,760
So a scientist shows up and has a press conference

202
01:68:13,760 --> 01:68:18,840
and says, I have figured out a way to turn corn into cars.

203
01:68:18,840 --> 01:68:20,000
And everyone laughs at him.

204
01:68:20,000 --> 01:68:21,280
That's ridiculous.

205
01:68:21,280 --> 01:68:23,680
The chemical components of corn would never

206
01:68:23,680 --> 01:68:26,840
allow it to be turned into cars.

207
01:68:26,840 --> 01:68:28,960
But then the guy goes and he buys an old factory

208
01:68:28,960 --> 01:68:30,160
down by the docks.

209
01:68:30,160 --> 01:68:34,080
And lo and behold, corn goes in, cars come out.

210
01:68:34,080 --> 01:68:36,640
So everyone says, wow, he has some new chemical process

211
01:68:36,640 --> 01:68:40,040
that no one else is smart enough to consider.

212
01:68:40,040 --> 01:68:41,240
Or happy.

213
01:68:41,240 --> 01:68:42,800
Until an investigative journalist

214
01:68:42,800 --> 01:68:46,600
sneaks into the factory and discovers it's only a dock.

215
01:68:46,600 --> 01:68:49,000
All that happens is ships with cars for Japan

216
01:68:49,000 --> 01:68:51,760
come in and leave full of corn.

217
01:68:51,760 --> 01:68:53,440
That's all that's occurring.

218
01:68:53,440 --> 01:68:54,960
And then the guy snaps some pictures,

219
01:68:54,960 --> 01:68:56,160
and there's a big scandal.

220
01:68:56,160 --> 01:68:58,240
And he says, corn into cars is a fraud.

221
01:68:58,240 --> 01:68:59,840
And then the scientist is arrested

222
01:68:59,840 --> 01:69:02,360
for violating every trade law in the books.

223
01:69:02,360 --> 01:69:04,920
The end.

224
01:69:04,920 --> 01:69:07,600
Now, the point of this thought experiment, of course,

225
01:69:07,600 --> 01:69:12,040
is that he did have a technology for turning corn into cars.

226
01:69:12,040 --> 01:69:13,720
And what is that technology called?

227
01:69:13,720 --> 01:69:15,880
It's called trade.

228
01:69:15,880 --> 01:69:17,400
And in fact, trade is a technology

229
01:69:17,400 --> 01:69:19,280
for turning anything into anything.

230
01:69:19,280 --> 01:69:21,800
It is like the transmuter from Star Trek.

231
01:69:21,800 --> 01:69:23,640
It is that amazing.

232
01:69:23,640 --> 01:69:25,880
The only way that it is different

233
01:69:25,880 --> 01:69:30,560
is that if it's a transmuter, then what happens

234
01:69:30,560 --> 01:69:33,800
is that Americans are better off and no one else is.

235
01:69:33,800 --> 01:69:35,480
But on the other hand, if there's trade,

236
01:69:35,480 --> 01:69:38,200
this means that the Japanese are better off as well.

237
01:69:38,200 --> 01:69:39,700
So from the American point of view,

238
01:69:39,700 --> 01:69:41,840
it doesn't matter how the factory works.

239
01:69:41,840 --> 01:69:43,280
But from the human point of view,

240
01:69:43,280 --> 01:69:44,800
it does matter because then there's

241
01:69:44,800 --> 01:69:47,440
another country where they are better off also,

242
01:69:47,440 --> 01:69:51,920
because they are receiving the other side of the deal.

243
01:69:51,920 --> 01:69:54,560
And I've got here a little question.

244
01:69:54,560 --> 01:69:56,920
Is anti-corn bearers an aestheticism

245
01:69:56,920 --> 01:70:00,000
of anti-free market economics?

246
01:70:00,000 --> 01:70:00,880
Well, let's see.

247
01:70:00,880 --> 01:70:02,640
I think of it as adding.

248
01:70:02,640 --> 01:70:05,680
So you have the general hostility to markets,

249
01:70:05,680 --> 01:70:07,480
and then there's additional hostility

250
01:70:07,480 --> 01:70:10,040
when there's foreigners involved,

251
01:70:10,040 --> 01:70:12,960
is at least the way that seems to me.

252
01:70:12,960 --> 01:70:13,460
Yes?

253
01:70:13,460 --> 01:70:15,760
So is the idea of corn for cars that nobody

254
01:70:15,760 --> 01:70:18,640
would object to corn for cars if they thought it was actually?

255
01:70:18,640 --> 01:70:22,680
Well, not literally nobody, but few.

256
01:70:22,680 --> 01:70:24,840
So there are actual Luddites who want

257
01:70:24,840 --> 01:70:27,880
to destroy new technologies, but that is a pretty small segment.

258
01:70:27,880 --> 01:70:29,920
There's not a lot of political attention

259
01:70:29,920 --> 01:70:32,400
being made in saying, let's smash the new machines.

260
01:70:32,400 --> 01:70:35,320
Whereas, let's go and cut off trade

261
01:70:35,320 --> 01:70:36,960
with a country that's destroying us.

262
01:70:36,960 --> 01:70:38,160
Much more popular.

263
01:70:38,160 --> 01:70:39,640
Because I feel like you could still

264
01:70:39,640 --> 01:70:42,920
set up a battle between the corn lobby

265
01:70:42,920 --> 01:70:46,520
and then better auto workers, because there would still be.

266
01:70:46,520 --> 01:70:47,020
Right.

267
01:70:47,020 --> 01:70:50,320
Like, what do people without a dog in the fight think?

268
01:70:50,320 --> 01:70:53,240
What does just a random American looking on the sidelines think?

269
01:70:53,240 --> 01:70:55,600
If someone says, there's a new machine that works great,

270
01:70:55,600 --> 01:70:58,120
it should be destroyed to save jobs,

271
01:70:58,120 --> 01:71:00,320
there's a little bit of appeal of that.

272
01:71:00,320 --> 01:71:02,800
But there's something like, I don't know,

273
01:71:02,800 --> 01:71:04,800
wouldn't we have to go and do other things too

274
01:71:04,800 --> 01:71:06,760
to be consistent if we believe that?

275
01:71:06,760 --> 01:71:08,960
Wouldn't we have to destroy our entire civilization?

276
01:71:11,680 --> 01:71:13,360
I don't think there's too much to spoil

277
01:71:13,360 --> 01:71:16,200
in saying the final episode of Battle Sword Galactica,

278
01:71:16,200 --> 01:71:19,840
they make a decision to destroy all their technology

279
01:71:19,840 --> 01:71:21,840
and then become cavemen.

280
01:71:21,840 --> 01:71:23,620
And then they become our ancestors.

281
01:71:23,620 --> 01:71:25,360
And just to make sure there's no evidence,

282
01:71:25,360 --> 01:71:26,920
they throw it all into the sun.

283
01:71:26,920 --> 01:71:28,960
So there's no sign of it ever happening.

284
01:71:28,960 --> 01:71:31,640
It sounds like a spoiler to me.

285
01:71:31,640 --> 01:71:36,600
Well, the show has been out and has been done for 10 years.

286
01:71:36,600 --> 01:71:38,840
